23:32:00.144 ***** GUARD PAGE ARMED: size class(es) 0x20+0x40 are served one-slot-per-page from a 64 GB reserve (16777216 slots) at 0x180060000 and DECOMMITTED on free, each address held back for 200s - one full firing of the writer's 180 s clock plus 20 s - before it can be handed out again. SIZED FROM THE 16:46 RUN OF 2026-09-16, which spent an 8388608-slot reserve in 254 s: 0x20 burst to 877768 in its first minute then ran at 34173/s (22x the 12:01 run it was sized from), 0x40 at 3396/s, and the two classes share ONE cursor. The 2 class(es) armed need 6358665 slots for their first retirement window and 7513800 once they are turning over; against the larger of those this reserve has 2.2x of headroom. 0x10 and 0x80 are unmeasured and modelled at 0x20's rate, so read the 'pool allocations seen by class' counters in the heartbeat - and read FELL BACK first: a fallen-back run looks exactly like a protected one. Metadata is committed lazily, so arming costs 65 MB now and grows with the cursor, which stops growing once recycling holds. Committed PAGE memory is bounded by the LIVE set, not by the cursor: 0x20 held ~54000 live slots all run, which is 210 MB, so expect roughly that per class. A stale write or read into a freed slot faults at the instruction that makes it, which the handler logs (RIP, target, who allocated, who freed, how long ago) and recommits so the client continues. control PASS. The pool's own chain, free list and counters are untouched - our slots take the HeapFree arm, and that arm is chosen by the header value ALONE, so one stamp 0x100 covers every class at once *****
